# PTO and Leave Policy
**Daisy Health**
**Policy ID:** HR-PT-001
**Effective Date:** January 1, 2025
**Last Reviewed:** June 1, 2025
**Owner:** People Operations

---

## Purpose

Daisy Health believes rest and recovery are essential to doing great work and delivering great care. This policy outlines how paid time off (PTO) works, how to request it, and what additional leave options are available.

---

## Scope

This policy applies to all full-time and part-time Daisy Health employees. Contractors are not eligible for PTO or company-sponsored leave.

---

## 1. PTO Accrual

Daisy Health uses an accrual-based PTO system. PTO accrues each pay period (bi-weekly) based on your role level and tenure.

| Level | Annual PTO | Accrual per Pay Period |
|---|---|---|
| Individual Contributor (0–2 years) | 15 days (120 hours) | 4.62 hours |
| Individual Contributor (3+ years) | 18 days (144 hours) | 5.54 hours |
| Senior / Clinical Lead (any tenure) | 20 days (160 hours) | 6.15 hours |
| Manager and above | 22 days (176 hours) | 6.77 hours |

PTO begins accruing on your first day of employment. Part-time employees accrue PTO on a prorated basis based on their scheduled hours.

---

## 2. PTO Usage

PTO may be used for any reason, including:

- Vacation and personal travel
- Illness or medical appointments (your own or a family member's)
- Mental health days
- Personal errands or obligations
- Any other personal need

### Minimum Increment

PTO may be taken in increments of **4 hours minimum** (half a day).

### First 30 Days

New employees may not take PTO during their first 30 days of employment without manager approval. Exceptions may be made for pre-planned travel disclosed during the hiring process.

### Notice Requirements

- **Planned PTO (vacation, travel):** Submit at least **5 business days in advance** through the HR portal
- **Unplanned PTO (illness, emergency):** Notify your manager as soon as possible, ideally before your shift begins
- **Extended PTO (5 or more consecutive days):** Submit at least **2 weeks in advance**

For clinical staff, last-minute PTO may require you to arrange shift coverage. See Section 7.

---

## 3. PTO Balances and Carryover

### Checking Your Balance

Your current PTO balance is visible in the HR portal at hr.daisyhealth.com at all times.

### Carryover

Employees may carry over up to **40 hours (5 days)** of unused PTO into the next calendar year. Any unused PTO above 40 hours is forfeited on December 31.

Daisy Health encourages employees to use their PTO throughout the year. Managers should support their teams in taking regular time off.

### PTO Payout at Separation

Upon separation from Daisy Health, employees will be paid out for unused accrued PTO up to the carryover maximum of 40 hours, in accordance with applicable state law.

---

## 4. Sick Leave

Sick leave is included within your PTO balance. There is no separate sick leave bank. Employees who are ill should use PTO for sick days.

If you are ill for **3 or more consecutive days**, you may be asked to provide a healthcare provider's note confirming you were seen and cleared to return to work. This requirement may be waived at manager discretion.

For extended illness (more than 5 consecutive days), you may be eligible for Short-Term Disability (STD) benefits. See Section 8.

---

## 5. How to Request PTO

All PTO requests are submitted through the **HR Portal** at hr.daisyhealth.com.

Steps:
1. Log in to the HR Portal
2. Navigate to Time Off > Request Time Off
3. Select the dates and type of leave
4. Add a note if needed (optional for PTO, required for leave types)
5. Submit — your manager will receive an automatic notification

Your manager must approve or deny PTO requests within **2 business days**. If you do not hear back within 2 business days, follow up directly with your manager.

PTO requests are generally approved unless business or patient care needs require a specific employee's presence. Managers may not deny PTO requests arbitrarily.

---

## 6. Manager Approval Guidelines

Managers are expected to:

- Review and respond to PTO requests within 2 business days
- Approve PTO when operationally feasible
- Communicate any PTO restrictions (blackout periods, coverage minimums) to their team in advance
- Not require employees to disclose the reason for PTO

Managers may deny PTO if:

- A minimum staffing level cannot be maintained during the requested period
- A critical deadline or project requires the employee's presence
- The employee is in their first 30 days without prior arrangement

Managers should not habitually deny or delay PTO approvals. Patterns of inappropriate denial should be reported to People Operations.

---

## 7. Clinical Staff PTO Considerations

Clinical staff have additional responsibilities around PTO because patient care coverage must be maintained.

- Clinical staff must **arrange shift coverage** for approved PTO before the absence
- The Clinical Operations team maintains a coverage pool and can help coordinate swaps
- PTO requests during **high-volume periods** (flu season, open enrollment periods) may be limited to maintain coverage levels
- Clinical blackout dates (if any) will be communicated by Clinical Operations at least **60 days in advance**

---

## 8. Leave of Absence Options

In addition to PTO, Daisy Health offers the following leave programs:

### Parental Leave

- **Primary caregiver:** 16 weeks fully paid leave for birth, adoption, or foster placement
- **Secondary caregiver:** 6 weeks fully paid leave
- Parental leave runs concurrently with any applicable FMLA entitlement
- Must be taken within 12 months of the child's birth, adoption, or placement
- Submit parental leave requests to People Operations at least 30 days before the anticipated start date

### Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA)

Employees who have worked at Daisy Health for at least **12 months** and at least **1,250 hours** in the past year are eligible for up to **12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave** per year for:

- Serious health condition of the employee
- Caring for a spouse, child, or parent with a serious health condition
- Birth, adoption, or foster placement of a child
- Qualifying military family needs

FMLA runs concurrently with paid leave (PTO, parental leave, STD). Contact People Operations to initiate an FMLA request.

### Short-Term Disability (STD)

Employees unable to work due to their own non-work-related illness, injury, or pregnancy recovery are eligible for STD after a **7-day waiting period**. STD provides **60% of your base salary** for up to 12 weeks. STD runs concurrently with FMLA where applicable.

### Bereavement Leave

- **Immediate family member** (spouse, child, parent, sibling): 5 paid days
- **Extended family member** (grandparent, aunt/uncle, in-law): 3 paid days
- **Close friend or colleague:** 1 paid day, at manager discretion
- Additional unpaid leave may be requested through People Operations

### Jury Duty

Employees summoned for jury duty receive full pay for up to **10 business days** of jury service. Provide your summons notice to People Operations and your manager as soon as possible.

---

## 9. Returning from Leave

Employees returning from FMLA or other protected leave are entitled to return to their same position or an equivalent position with the same pay, benefits, and terms of employment.

Employees returning from leave of 2 weeks or more should coordinate with their manager and People Operations at least 5 days before their return date to ensure system access, equipment, and scheduling are ready.

